<?xpacket end="w"?>
endstreamendobj536 0 obj<</A 537 0 R/Alt(46.4% of families/carers of 2019-20 entrants aged 0 to 14 were working in a paid job, compared to 75.2% of Australians aged 25 to 64. The population percentage is from ABS Labour Force Statistics, Original Series, as at 30 June 2020. About 90% of responde\nts are female \(mothers\) and are likely to be at the younger end of the age range. For comparison, weighting by gender and restricting to age range 25 to 49 would give a benchmark of 74.2%, rather than 75.2%.\r\n81.4% of those in a paid job work 15 hour\s or more per week.\r\n46.5% of families/carers of 2019-20 entrants aged 0 to 14 said that they \(and/or their partner\) were able to work as much as they wanted.\r\nOf the families/carers who did not work as much as they liked, 17.9% said that the availa\bility of jobs was a barrier, 32.2% said that insufficient flexibility of jobs was a barrier, and 86.2% identified the situation of their child with disability as a barrier to working more.\r\n␀)/K[539 0 R 30]/P 518 0 R/Pg 360 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj550 0 obj<</A 551 0 R/Alt(In relation to the statement I know what specialist services are needed to promote my childs learning and development, 37.4% reported yes, 55.3% stated that they knew to some degree, and 7.4% reported no. \r\nWhen asked, I know what my family can do to s\upport my childs learning and development, 37.4% reported yes, 55.9% answered to some degree, and 6.7% reported no. \r\nIn regards to the statement, I get enough support to feel confident in parenting my child with disability, 44.3% of family/carers repo\rted yes, 44.5% stated to some degree, and 11.3% reported no.\r\nOverall, 25.8% of family/carers felt very confident in supporting their childs development, 61.0% felt somewhat confident, 11.6% did not feel very confident, and 1.5% did not feel at all co\nfident.\r\n␀)/K[553 0 R 44]/P 542 0 R/Pg 362 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj621 0 obj<</A 622 0 R/Alt(54.2% of families/carers of 2019-20 entrants aged 15 to 24 were working in a paid job, compared to 75.2% of Australians aged 25 to 64. The population percentage is from ABS Labour Force Statistics, Original Series, as at 30 June 2020. 80% of respondents a\re mothers, 12% are fathers, 2% are grandmothers, 6% are others. For comparison, weighting by gender would give a benchmark of 71.4%, rather than 75.2%.\r\n86.4% of those in a paid job work 15 hours or more per week.\r\n48.2% of families/carers of 2019-20\ entrants aged 15 to 24 said that they \(and/or their partner\) were able to work as much as they wanted.\r\nOf the families/carers who did not work as much as they liked, 15.6% said that the availability of jobs was a barrier, 25.7% said that insufficien\t flexibility of jobs was a barrier, and 90.7% identified the situation with their family member with disability as a barrier to working more.\r\n␀)/K[624 0 R 28]/P 602 0 R/Pg 376 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj635 0 obj<</A 636 0 R/Alt(When asked, I know what my family can do to enable my family member with disability to become as independent as possible, 35.9% reported yes, 48.3% answered I have some difficulty, and 15.8% reported I have a great deal of difficulty. \r\nIn regards to th\e statement, I enable/support my family member with disability to make more decisions in his/her life, 54.2% of family/carers reported yes, 34.2% stated I have some difficulty, and 11.6% reported having a great deal of difficulty.\r\nIn relation to the st\atement I enable/support my family member with disability to interact and develop strong relationships with non-family members, 42.2% reported yes, 38.5% stated I have some difficulty, and 19.3% reported having a great deal of difficulty. \r\n␀)/K[638 0 R 29]/P 627 0 R/Pg 378 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj705 0 obj<</A 706 0 R/Alt(40.0% of families/carers of 2019-20 entrants aged 25 and over were working in a paid job, compared to 77.6% of Australians aged 25 to 64. The population percentage is from ABS Labour Force Statistics, Original Series, as at 30 June 2020. 38% of respondent\s are spouses/partners, 30% are mothers, 6% are fathers, 8% are sisters, 3% are brothers, 9% are other family members and 5% are others.\r\n86.1% of those in a paid job work 15 hours or more per week.\r\n51.7% of families/carers of 2019-20 entrants aged 2\5 and over said that they \(and/or their partner\) were able to work as much as they wanted.\r\nOf the families/carers who did not work as much as they liked, 12.3% said that the availability of jobs was a barrier, 18.8% said that insufficient flexibility\ of jobs was a barrier, and 90.1% identified the situation with their family member with disability as a barrier to working more.\r\n␀)/K[708 0 R 27]/P 687 0 R/Pg 105 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj720 0 obj<</A 721 0 R/Alt(When asked if they had made plans for when they are no longer able to care for their family member with a disability, 70.2% stated no, 21.3% stated that they have begun, and 8.5% stated yes.  \r\nOf those families/carers who had commenced succession plann\ing and asked for assistance, 36.3% asked siblings, 31.0% asked extended family, 29.5% asked service providers, 29.2% asked professionals, 16.5% asked support workers, 9.3% asked friends of the family/carer, and 6.5% asked friends of the participant.\r\n␀\)/C/CM76/ID(AD000000-0000-0000-ADBE-      103573)/K[723 0 R 6]/P 715 0 R/Pg 115 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj1166 0 obj<</A 1167 0 R/Alt(By age, 13.9% of 2019-20 entrants were 2 or younger, compared to 7.3% for prior year entrants. 40.8% of 2019-20 entrants were 3 to 5, compared to 29.3% for prior year entrants. 19.6% of 2019-20 entrants were 6 to 8, compared to 28.1% for prior year entran\ts. 14.0% of 2019-20 entrants were 9 to 11, compared to 18.7% for prior year entrants. 11.7% of 2019-20 entrants were 12 or older, compared to 16.5% for prior year entrants. \r\nBy disability, 45.3% of 2019-20 entrants have Autism, compared to 51.4% for p\rior year entrants. 36.1% of 2019-20 entrants have Developmental delay/Global developmental delay, compared to 18.6% for prior year entrants. 7.0% of 2019-20 entrants have Intellectual disability/Down syndrome, compared to 13.8% for prior year entrants. 7\.5% of 2019-20 entrants have Sensory disability, similar to prior year entrants. 4.2% of 2019-20 entrants have an Other disability type, compared to 8.6% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy level of function, 55.0% of 2019-20 entrants have a high level of func\tion, compared to 50.1% for prior year entrants. 34.7% of 2019-20 entrants have a medium level of function, compared to 32.4% for prior year entrants. 10.3% of 2019-20 entrants have a low level of function, compared to 17.5% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy\ level of NDIA support, 61.9% of 2019-20 entrants require a low level of NDIA support, compared to 37.6% for prior year entrants. 32.0% of 2019-20 entrants require a medium level of NDIA support, compared to 52.2% for prior year entrants. 6.1% of 2019-20 \entrants require a high/very high level of NDIA support, compared to 10.2% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy State/Territory, 26.0% of 2019-20 entrants live in New South Wales, compared to 37.8% for prior year entrants. 30.5% of 2019-20 entrants live in Vict\oria, compared to 28.0% for prior year entrants. 23.3% of 2019-20 entrants live in Queensland, compared to 17.1% for prior year entrants. 9.9% of 2019-20 entrants live in Western Australia, compared to 5.1% for prior year entr␀)/C[/CM2/CM76]/ID(AD000000-0000-0000-ADBE-      104192)/K[1169 0 R 103]/P 1165 0 R/Pg 128 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj1171 0 obj<</A 1172 0 R/Alt(By Indigenous status, 8.2% of 2019-20 entrants identified as Indigenous, compared to 6.9% for prior year entrants. 77.9% of 2019-20 entrants identified as non-Indigenous, compared to 70.3% for prior year entrants. 13.9% of 2019-20 entrants did not identif\y their Indigenous status, compared to 22.8% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy CALD background, 9.0% of 2019-20 entrants are from a CALD background, compared to 7.2% for prior year entrants. 90.9% of 2019-20 entrants are from a non-CALD background, compared \to 90.9% for prior year entrants. 0.1% of 2019-20 entrants have CALD-background not stated, compared to 1.9% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy gender, 30.4% of 2019-20 entrants are female, compared to 29.1% for prior year entrants. 68.5% of 2019-20 entrants \are male, compared to 69.4% for prior year entrants. 1.1% of 2019-20 entrants are gender unknown, compared to 1.6% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy Scheme entry type, 78.8% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme as new, compared to 43.7% for prior year entr\ants. 14.2% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme from a State program, compared to 45.2% for prior year entrants. 7.0% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme from a Commonwealth program, compared to 11.1% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy access request de\cision, 51.6% of 2019-20 entrants met access under benefit from EI, compared to 34.5% for prior year entrants. 48.4% of 2019-20 entrants met access under disability met, compared to 65.5% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy annualised plan budget, 13.4% of 201\9-20 entrants have a budget of $10,000 or less, compared to 25.0% for prior year entrants. 25.7% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $10-15,000, compared to 31.7% for prior year entrants. 29.5% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $15-20,000, compared to\ 19.4% for prior year entrants. 23.2% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $20-$30,000, compared to 11.3% for prior year entrants. 8.2% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of over $30,000, compared to 12.6% for prior year entrants.␀)/K[1174 0 R 104]/P 1170 0 R/Pg 131 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj1240 0 obj<</A 1241 0 R/Alt(The percentages of family/carer respondents who reported receiving both the carer payment and carer allowance was 7.6%, the carer payment only was 5.9%, the carer allowance only was 22.8%, and neither was 63.7%.␀)/ID(AD000000-0000-0000-ADBE-      104815)/K[1242 0 R 10]/P 1233 0 R/Pg 135 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj1259 0 obj<</A 1260 0 R/Alt(In relation to selected outcomes in the Rights and Advocacy domain for families/carers of participants aged 0 to 14 who entered the Scheme in 2019-20: 61.9% are able to identify the needs of their child and family, 72.2% understand their rights and the ri\ghts of their child, 77.3% are able to advocate for their child, 56.4% had difficulty accessing available services and supports to meet the needs of their child and family, and 56.1% experienced at least one barrier to access or advocacy. \r\nWhen asked a\bout barriers experienced, 43.9% reported none. Of those who experienced barriers, the prevalence of each type of barrier was: limited access to information and resources 35.2%, lack of knowledge or understanding about my rights/the rights of my child 21.\6%, time constraints \(i.e. not enough time to look into or pursue\) 19.4%, lack of support 26.8%, fear of potential consequences 4.9%, and other 7.7%.\r\n␀)/K[1262 0 R 17]/P 1257 0 R/Pg 144 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj1285 0 obj<</A 1286 0 R/Alt(The percentages in each self-rated health category were: 36.0% excellent/very good for families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14, compared with 57.0% for Australians aged 25 and over. 41.2% good for families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14,\ compared with 28.0% for Australians aged 25 and over. 22.9% fair/poor for families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14, compared with 15.1% for Australians aged 25 and over.␀)/K[1288 0 R 31]/P 1284 0 R/Pg 149 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj1293 0 obj<</A 1294 0 R/Alt(In terms of outlook for the future, 5.8% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt delighted, compared to 14.0% for Australians aged 25 and over. 23.1% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt pleased, compared to 29.0% for \Australians aged 25 and over. 23.7% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt mostly satisfied, compared to 34.0% for Australians aged 25 and over. 34.5% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt mixed, compared to 17.0% for \Australians aged 25 and over. 2.3% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt mostly dissatisfied, compared to 2.1% for Australians aged 25 and over. 2.3% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt unhappy, compared to 1.9% for\ Australians aged 25 and over. 1.9% of families and carers of participants aged 0 to 14 felt terrible, compared to 1.0% for Australians aged 25 and over.␀)/K[1296 0 R 35]/P 1284 0 R/Pg 149 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj4860 0 obj<</A 4861 0 R/Alt(There was a decrease in the percentage of families/carers in a paid job at the COVID-19 date \(i.e. 23 March 2020\). The fitted trend line was just below 60% pre-COVID period and between 50% and 60% from the start of the pandemic. However, for those in a \paid job at the COVID-19 date, there was an increase in the percentage working 15 hours or more per week. The fitted trend line was just above 80% pre-COVID period and slightly higher from the start of the pandemic.\r\nThe percentage who say that they \(a\nd/or their partner\) are able to work as much as they want increased more strongly from the COVID-19 date \(i.e. 23 March 2020\). The fitted trend line was slightly trending up from 50% pre-COVID period. The fitted trend line from the start of the pandem\ic went from just above 50% at 23 March 2020 to just below 60% at 1 July 2020.\r\nOf those unable to work as much as they want, the percentage who say that availability of jobs is a barrier to working more rose to a higher constant level during the pandem\ic … the fitted trend line was just above 20% for the pre-COVID period and increased slightly from the start of the pandemic. The percentage saying the situation of their child with disability was a barrier dropped to a lower constant level from just abov\e 80% pre-COVID to between 70% and 80% at the start of the pandemic.\r\nThe percentage receiving Carer Allowance had been declining over time prior to the pandemic, but this decreasing trend levelled off during the pandemic. The fitted trend line was at j\ust above 20% pre-COVID declining to between 10% and 20% at the start of the pandemic. ␀)/C[/CM30/CM74]/ID(AD000000-0000-0000-ADBE-      108156)/K[4863 0 R 1]/P 4859 0 R/Pg 219 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj4879 0 obj<</A 4880 0 R/Alt(For the four indicators related to families feeling supported \(i.e. having friends they can see as often as they like; having people they can ask for practical help, childcare, and emotional support as often as they need\): there was a general increasing\ trend in these indicators prior to the start of the pandemic \(i.e. before 23 March 2020\), a significant step increase at the start date, and a continuation of the same increasing trend post-onset of the pandemic. The fitted trend line percentages for t\hese indicators pre-COVID ranged from just below 40% to around 70%. \r\nFor the two indicators related to families/carers being supported in helping their child develop and learn \(getting enough support in parenting their child, and feeling confident in \supporting their childs development\): there was a significant improvement in these indicators to a new constant level post-onset of the pandemic. The fitted trend line percentages for these indicators pre-COVID were between 50% and 60% for the support i\ndicator and about 90% for the level of confidence indicator.␀)/K[4882 0 R 38]/P 4877 0 R/Pg 222 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj4895 0 obj<</A 4896 0 R/Alt(The percentage of families/carers and their partners who are able to engage in social interactions and community life as much as they want has continued to increase post 23 March 2020, but at a higher rate than before 23 March 2020. The fitted trend line \was between 30% and 40% pre-COVID increasing to about 40% at 23 March 2020 and further increasing to just below 50% at 1 July 2020.\r\nFor those unable to engage as much as they want, the percentage who say the situation of their child with disability is \a barrier to engaging more dropped to a lower constant level during the pandemic. The fitted trend line was up towards 90% pre-COVID dropping towards 80% at 23 March 2020.\r\nThe percentage of families/carers rating their health as excellent, very good or\ good rose to a new constant level during the pandemic. The fitted trend line was just above 80% pre-COVID increasing closer to 90% at the start of the pandemic.␀)/C/CM5/ID(AD000000-0000-0000-ADBE-      108454)/K[4898 0 R 6]/P 4892 0 R/Pg 224 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj5084 0 obj<</A 5085 0 R/Alt(By age, 51.7% of 2019-20 entrants were 17 or less, compared to 45.1% for prior year entrants. 33.6% of 2019-20 entrants were 18 to 21, compared to 35.8% for prior year entrants. 14.7% of 2019-20 entrants were 22 or above, compared to 19.1% for prior year \entrants.\r\nBy disability type, 50.3% of 2019-20 entrants have Autism, compared to 40.3% for prior year entrants. 26.3% of 2019-20 entrants have Intellectual Disability/Down syndrome, compared to 41.1% for prior year entrants. 11.2% of 2019-20 entrants h\ave a hearing/visual impairment, compared to 4.0% for prior year entrants. 3.7% of 2019-20 entrants have a psychosocial disability, compared to 1.9% for prior year entrants. 4.5% of 2019-20 entrants have cerebral palsy/other neurological disability, compa\red to 8.5% for prior year entrants. 4.0% of 2019-20 entrants have Other disability, compared to 4.2% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy level of function, 21.2% of 2019-20 entrants have a high level of function, compared to 17.7% for prior year entrants. 57.\4% of 2019-20 entrants have a medium level of function, compared to 50.7% for prior year entrants. 21.5% of 2019-20 entrants have a low level of function, compared to 31.6% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy level of NDIA support, 49.6% of 2019-20 entrants re\quire a low level of NDIA support, compared to 23.8% for prior year entrants. 36.1% of 2019-20 entrants require a medium level of NDIA support, compared to 56.2% for prior year entrants. 14.3% of 2019-20 entrants require a high/very high level of NDIA sup\port, compared to 20.0% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy State/Territory, 21.9% of 2019-20 entrants live in New South Wales, compared to 30.5% for prior year entrants. 26.9% of 2019-20 entrants live in Victoria, compared to 29.0% for prior year entrants. 20\.5% of 2019-20 entrants live in Queensland, compared to 21.0% for prior year entrants. 22.0% of 2019-20 entrants live in Western Australia, compared to 7.3% for prior year entrants. 5.4% of 2019-20 entrants live in South Austr␀)/K[5086 0 R 106]/P 5083 0 R/Pg 241 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj5089 0 obj<</A 5090 0 R/Alt(By Indigenous status, 6.7% of 2019-20 entrants identified as Indigenous, compared to 6.2% for prior year entrants. 79.8% of 2019-20 entrants identified as non-Indigenous, compared to 74.7% for prior year entrants. 13.4% of 2019-20 entrants did not state, \compared to 19.1% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy CALD background, 9.1% of 2019-20 entrants are from a CALD background, compared to 6.6% for prior year entrants. 90.8% of 2019-20 entrants are from a non-CALD background, compared to 90.4% for prior year ent\rants. 0.1% of 2019-20 entrants are from a CALD background not stated, compared to 3.0% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy gender, 37.2% of 2019-20 entrants are female, compared to 34.6% for prior year entrants. 61.3% of 2019-20 entrants are male, compared to\ 64.5% for prior year entrants. 1.5% of 2019-20 entrants are gender unknown, compared to 0.8% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy Scheme entry type, 70.5% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme as new, compared to 28.9% for prior year entrants. 27.5% of 2019-2\0 entrants entered the Scheme from a State program, compared to 68.9% for prior year entrants. 1.9% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme from a Commonwealth program, compared to 2.2% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy access request decision, 17.5% of 2019-\20 entrants met access under benefit from EI, compared to 9.5% for prior year entrants. 82.5% of 2019-20 entrants met access under disability met, compared to 90.5% for prior year entrants. \r\nBy annualised plan budget, 23.2% of 2019-20 entrants have a b\udget of $15,000 or less, compared to 18.3% for prior year entrants. 31.3% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $15-30,000, compared to 24.8% for prior year entrants. 23.3% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $30-50,000, compared to 20.9% for prior year \entrants. 22.2% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of over $50,000, compared to 36.0% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy baseline plan management type, 22.2% of 2019-20 entrants were self managed fully, compared to 12.0% for prior year e␀)/K[5091 0 R 134]/P 5088 0 R/Pg 243 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj5181 0 obj<</A 5182 0 R/Alt(The percentages of family/carer respondents who reported receiving both the carer payment and carer allowance was 10.5%, the carer payment only was 8.3%, the carer allowance only was 23.3%, and neither was 57.9%.␀)/ID 5183 0 R/K[5184 0 R 1]/P 5180 0 R/Pg 247 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj5197 0 obj<</A 5198 0 R/Alt(In relation to feeling supported outcomes:\r\nFor those who have friends and family they can see as often as they like: 68% have people they can ask for practical help as often as they need, 56% have people they can ask to support the participant, and 78%\ have people they can ask for emotional support.\r\nFor those who do not have friends and family they can see as often as they like: 12% have people they can ask for practical help as often as they need, 8% have people they can ask to support the particip\ant, and 29% have people they can ask for emotional support.\r\n␀)/K[5199 0 R 19]/P 5195 0 R/Pg 249 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj5227 0 obj<</A 5228 0 R/Alt(The percentages in each self-rated health category were: 23.0% excellent/very good for families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24, compared with 51.1% for Australians aged 40 to 69. 42.3% good for families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24, \compared with 31.2% for Australians aged 40 to 69. 34.7% fair/poor for families and carers of participants aged 15 to 24, compared with 17.7% for Australians aged 40 to 69.␀)/K[5229 0 R 28]/P 5214 0 R/Pg 251 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj5335 0 obj<</A 5336 0 R/Alt(51% of respondents who have a family member with Psychosocial disability rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and 42% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n62% of respondents who have a family member with Autism rated their h\ealth as excellent, very good or good, and 44% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n65% of respondents who have a family member with Intellectual disability rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and 48% reported being able to\ work as much as they want.\r\nOverall, 65% of respondents who have a family member with a disability rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and 48% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n70% of respondents who have a family mem\ber with Down syndrome rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and 46% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n71% of respondents who have a family member with Cerebral palsy rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and\ 48% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n77% of respondents who have a family member with Visual impairment rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and 56% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n83% of respondent\s who have a family member with Hearing impairment rated their health as excellent, very good or good, and 74% reported being able to work as much as they want.\r\n␀)/C[/CM44_1/CM79_1]/ID 5337 0 R/K[5338 0 R 1]/P 5333 0 R/Pg 258 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj8446 0 obj<</A 8447 0 R/Alt(For the indicator … having friends they can see as often as they like, there was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was just below 40% pre-COVID period and just above 40% from the start of the \pandemic.\r\nFor the indicator … having people they can ask to support their family member with disability as often as they need, there was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was between 20% an\d 30% pre-COVID period and just below 30% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor the indicator … having people they can talk to for emotional support as often as they need, there was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positiv\e. The fitted trend line was just below 50% pre-COVID period and between 50% and 60% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor the indicator … feeling that the services they use for their family member with disability listen to them, there was a step change \at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was just above 60% pre-COVID period and just below 70% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor families/carers that rated their health as excellent, very good or good, there\ was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was just below 60% pre-COVID period and just above 60% from the start of the pandemic.\r\n␀)/ID 8448 0 R/K[8449 0 R 1]/P 8444 0 R/Pg 293 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj8745 0 obj<</A 8746 0 R/Alt(By age, 21.1% of 2019-20 entrants were 34 or younger, compared to 27.4% for prior year entrants. 18.8% of 2019-20 entrants were 35 to 44, compared to 22.1% for prior year entrants. 22.9% of 2019-20 entrants were 45 to 54, and 22.9% for prior year entrants\. 37.1% of 2019-20 entrants were 55 or over, compared to 27.6% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy disability, 19.0% of 2019-20 entrants have a psychosocial disability, compared to 10.9% for prior year entrants. 17.3% of 2019-20 entrants have Intellectual Disa\bility/Down syndrome, compared to 35.1% for prior year entrants. 14.0% of 2019-20 entrants have ABI/Stroke, compared to 11.5% for prior year entrants. 12.8% of 2019-20 entrants have a spinal cord injury/other physical disability, compared to 8.9% for prio\r year entrants. 17.5% of 2019-20 entrants have cerebral palsy/other neurological disability, compared to 15.2% for prior year entrants. 19.4% of 2019-20 entrants have Other disability type, compared to 18.5% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy level of functi\on, 11.4% of 2019-20 entrants have a high level of function, compared to 11.7% for prior year entrants. 51.9% of 2019-20 entrants have a medium level of function, compared to 52.0% for prior year entrants. 36.7% of 2019-20 entrants have a low level of fun\ction, compared to 36.2% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy level of NDIA support, 32.1% of 2019-20 entrants require a low level of NDIA support, compared to 17.4% for prior year entrants. 34.0% of 2019-20 entrants require a medium level of NDIA support, comp\ared to 50.3% for prior year entrants. 33.8% of 2019-20 entrants require a high/very high level of NDIA support, compared to 32.3% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy State/Territory, 23.0% of 2019-20 entrants live in New South Wales, compared to 24.6% for pri\or year entrants. 24.4% of 2019-20 entrants live in Victoria, compared to 32.2% for prior year entrants. 19.7% of 2019-20 entrants live in Queensland, compared to 20.9% for prior year entrants. 20.2% of 2019-20 entrants live i␀)/K[8747 0 R 95]/P 8744 0 R/Pg 310 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj8751 0 obj<</A 8752 0 R/Alt(By Indigenous status, 5.3% of 2019-20 entrants identified as Indigenous, compared to 3.9% for prior year entrants. 80.7% of 2019-20 entrants identified as non-Indigenous, compared to 78.5% for prior year entrants. 14.0% of 2019-20 entrants identified did \not state, compared to 17.6% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy CALD background, 17.7% of 2019-20 entrants are from a CALD background, compared to 11.9% for prior year entrants. 82.2% of 2019-20 entrants are from a non-CALD background, compared to 85.8% for p\rior year entrants. 0.1% of 2019-20 entrants are from a CALD background not stated, compared to 2.2% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy gender, 43.5% of 2019-20 entrants are female, compared to 43.7% for prior year entrants. 55.3% of 2019-20 entrants are male\, compared to 55.3% for prior year entrants. 1.2% of 2019-20 entrants are gender unknown, compared to 1.0% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy Scheme entry type, 70.8% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme as new, compared to 28.3% for prior year entrants. 19\.5% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme from a State program, compared to 60.3% for prior year entrants. 9.6% of 2019-20 entrants entered the Scheme from a Commonwealth program, compared to 11.5% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy access request decision, \4.2% of 2019-20 entrants met access under benefit from EI, compared to 2.2% for prior year entrants. 95.8% of 2019-20 entrants met access under disability met, compared to 97.8% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy annualised plan budget, 8.4% of 2019-20 entran\ts have a budget of $15,000 or less, compared to 9.5% for prior year entrants. 14.9% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $15-30,000, compared to 15.6% for prior year entrants. 22.9% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $30-50,000, compared to 20.2% for p\rior year entrants. 31.5% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of $50-100,000, compared to 27.8% for prior year entrants. 22.3% of 2019-20 entrants have a budget of over $100,000, compared to 27.0% for prior year entrants.\r\nBy baseli␀)/C/CM76_1/ID 8753 0 R/K[8754 0 R 1]/P 8750 0 R/Pg 312 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj8851 0 obj<</A 8852 0 R/Alt(The percentages of family/carer respondents who reported receiving both the carer payment and carer allowance was 11.4%, the carer payment only was 8.1%, the carer allowance only was 15.1%, and neither was 65.3%.␀)/ID 8853 0 R/K[8854 0 R 1]/P 8849 0 R/Pg 316 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj8883 0 obj<</A 8884 0 R/Alt(When asked if they had made plans for when they are no longer able to care for their family member with a disability, 70.2% stated no, 21.3% stated that they have begun, and 8.5% stated yes.  \r\nOf those families/carers who had commenced succession plann\ing and asked for assistance, 36.3% asked siblings, 31.0% asked extended family, 29.5% asked service providers, 29.2% asked professionals, 16.5% asked support workers, 9.3% asked friends of the family/carer, and 6.5% asked friends of the participant.\r\n␀\)/K[8885 0 R 12]/P 8869 0 R/Pg 319 0 R/S/Figure>>endobj10882 0 obj<</A 10883 0 R/Alt(For families/carers who have a paid job, the percentage working 15 hours or more per week increased. There was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was between 70% and 80% pre-COVID period and ju\st below 80% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor families/carers providing informal care to their family member with disability that are able to work as much as they want, there was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more posit\ive. The fitted trend line was between 60% and 70% pre-COVID period and just below 70% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor the indicator … having people they can talk to for emotional support as often as they need, there was a step change at 23 March 2\020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was at 50% pre-COVID period and just above 50% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor the indicator … feeling that the services they use for their family member with disability listen t\o them, there was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was at 60% pre-COVID period and just above 60% from the start of the pandemic.\r\nFor families/carers who have begun to make plans for the f\uture support of their family member, the percentage who have asked for help from service providers, professionals or support workers decreased. The fitted trend line was just below 40% pre-COVID period and just above 30% from the start of the pandemic.\r\\nFor families/carers that rated their health as excellent, very good or good, there was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was just above 40% pre-COVID period and between 40% and 50% from the \start of the pandemic.␀)/K[10884 0 R 22]/P 10880 0 R/Pg 345 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj10888 0 obj<</A 10889 0 R/Alt(A higher percentage of families/carers thought that their family member with disability gets the support they need. There was a step change at 23 March 2020, with the indicator becoming more positive. The fitted trend line was just above 20% pre-COVID per\iod and just below 30% from the start of the pandemic.␀)/K[10890 0 R 6]/P 10886 0 R/Pg 347 0 R/S/Figure>>endobjxref
